Feature: Creation of activity_final_log table in Unity Catalog purgo_databricks.purgo_playground

  The activity_final_log table is created from AWS.entity_activity_log and AWS.priority_weight_settings
  with specific filtering, joining, and transformation logic as per the "Final Act Log" mapping specification.

  Background:
    Given the source table AWS.entity_activity_log exists and is accessible
    And the source table AWS.priority_weight_settings exists and is accessible
    And the target table activity_final_log does not exist in purgo_databricks.purgo_playground
    And the following columns are required in activity_final_log:
      | Column Name      | Data Type | Description                                                                 |
      | usr_id           | string    | Distinct modified_by_user_id from entity_activity_log                       |
      | usr_name         | string    | Distinct modified_by_user_name associated with usr_id                       |
      | date             | date      | Distinct Date (activity_closed_at) associated with usr_id & usr_name        |
      | activity_bucket  | string    | Distinct priority_level_desc from priority_weight_settings for usr_id, date |
      | new_act_flag     | string    | 'New' or 'Continuing' based on cutoff_timestamp and activity_closed_at      |

  Scenario: Create activity_final_log table with correct schema
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed to create activity_final_log table
    Then the table activity_final_log should exist in purgo_databricks.purgo_playground
    And the table should have columns:
      | usr_id          | string |
      | usr_name        | string |
      | date            | date   |
      | activity_bucket | string |
      | new_act_flag    | string |

  Scenario Outline: Insert valid records into activity_final_log with correct transformation and filtering
    Given a record in AWS.entity_activity_log with:
      | modified_by_user_id     | <usr_id>         |
      | modified_by_user_name   | <usr_name>       |
      | activity_closed_at      | <activity_closed_at> |
      | activity_subcategory    | <activity_subcategory> |
      | modified_by_user_role   | <modified_by_user_role> |
      | interaction_type        | <interaction_type> |
      | subject                 | <subject>        |
      | cutoff_timestamp        | <cutoff_timestamp> |
    And a record in AWS.priority_weight_settings with:
      | task_category           | <task_category>  |
      | priority_level_desc     | <priority_level_desc> |
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then a record should be inserted into activity_final_log with:
      | usr_id          | <usr_id>                |
      | usr_name        | <usr_name>              |
      | date            | <activity_closed_at_date> |
      | activity_bucket | <priority_level_desc>    |
      | new_act_flag    | <expected_new_act_flag>  |

    Examples:
      | usr_id | usr_name | activity_closed_at     | activity_subcategory | modified_by_user_role | interaction_type | subject   | cutoff_timestamp | task_category | priority_level_desc | activity_closed_at_date | expected_new_act_flag |
      | U123   | Alice    | 2025-01-02T10:00:00Z  | Task                | PN                   | C&R             | Billing   | 2025-01-02       | Billing       | High               | 2025-01-02             | New                  |
      | U124   | Bob      | 2025-01-03T12:00:00Z  | Task                | PN                   | PF              | Support   | NULL             | Support       | Medium             | 2025-01-03             | New                  |
      | U125   | Carol    | 2025-01-04T09:00:00Z  | Task                | PN                   | C&R             | Claims    | 2025-01-03       | Claims        | Low                | 2025-01-04             | Continuing           |

  Scenario Outline: Do not insert records that fail filter criteria
    Given a record in AWS.entity_activity_log with:
      | modified_by_user_id     | <usr_id>         |
      | modified_by_user_name   | <usr_name>       |
      | activity_closed_at      | <activity_closed_at> |
      | activity_subcategory    | <activity_subcategory> |
      | modified_by_user_role   | <modified_by_user_role> |
      | interaction_type        | <interaction_type> |
      | subject                 | <subject>        |
      | cutoff_timestamp        | <cutoff_timestamp> |
    And a record in AWS.priority_weight_settings with:
      | task_category           | <task_category>  |
      | priority_level_desc     | <priority_level_desc> |
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then no record should be inserted into activity_final_log for this input

    Examples:
      | usr_id | usr_name | activity_closed_at     | activity_subcategory | modified_by_user_role | interaction_type | subject   | cutoff_timestamp | task_category | priority_level_desc |
      | U126   | Dave     | 2024-12-31T23:59:59Z  | Task                | PN                   | C&R             | Billing   | 2025-01-02       | Billing       | High               |  # activity_closed_at before 2025-01-01
      | U127   | Eve      | NULL                  | Task                | PN                   | PF              | Support   | NULL             | Support       | Medium             |  # activity_closed_at is NULL
      | U128   | Frank    | 2025-01-05T10:00:00Z  | Case                | PN                   | C&R             | Claims    | 2025-01-03       | Claims        | Low                |  # activity_subcategory != 'Task'
      | U129   | Grace    | 2025-01-06T11:00:00Z  | Task                | Admin                | PF              | Billing   | 2025-01-06       | Billing       | High               |  # modified_by_user_role != 'PN'
      | U130   | Heidi    | 2025-01-07T12:00:00Z  | Task                | PN                   | Email           | Support   | 2025-01-07       | Support       | Medium             |  # interaction_type not in ('C&R', 'PF')

  Scenario Outline: Error handling for join mismatches
    Given a record in AWS.entity_activity_log with:
      | modified_by_user_id     | <usr_id>         |
      | modified_by_user_name   | <usr_name>       |
      | activity_closed_at      | <activity_closed_at> |
      | activity_subcategory    | Task             |
      | modified_by_user_role   | PN               |
      | interaction_type        | C&R              |
      | subject                 | <subject>        |
      | cutoff_timestamp        | <cutoff_timestamp> |
    And no matching record in AWS.priority_weight_settings for subject = <subject>
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then the activity_bucket field in activity_final_log should be NULL for this record

    Examples:
      | usr_id | usr_name | activity_closed_at     | subject   | cutoff_timestamp |
      | U131   | Ivan     | 2025-01-08T13:00:00Z  | Unknown   | 2025-01-08      |
      | U132   | Judy     | 2025-01-09T14:00:00Z  | Missing   | NULL            |

  Scenario Outline: Validation of new_act_flag logic
    Given a record in AWS.entity_activity_log with:
      | activity_closed_at      | <activity_closed_at> |
      | cutoff_timestamp        | <cutoff_timestamp>   |
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then the new_act_flag field should be set as follows:
      | If cutoff_timestamp is NULL then 'New'
      | Else if activity_closed_at <= cutoff_timestamp then 'New'
      | Else 'Continuing'

    Examples:
      | activity_closed_at     | cutoff_timestamp | expected_new_act_flag |
      | 2025-01-10T10:00:00Z  | NULL            | New                  |
      | 2025-01-11T09:00:00Z  | 2025-01-11      | New                  |
      | 2025-01-12T12:00:00Z  | 2025-01-11      | Continuing           |

  Scenario: Data type validation for activity_final_log columns
    Given the activity_final_log table is created
    When a record is inserted
    Then the data types of the columns should be:
      | usr_id          | string |
      | usr_name        | string |
      | date            | date   |
      | activity_bucket | string |
      | new_act_flag    | string |

  Scenario Outline: Error scenario for invalid data formats
    Given a record in AWS.entity_activity_log with:
      | activity_closed_at      | <activity_closed_at> |
      | cutoff_timestamp        | <cutoff_timestamp>   |
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then an error should be raised with message <error_message>

    Examples:
      | activity_closed_at     | cutoff_timestamp | error_message                                      |
      | invalid_date           | 2025-01-13      | "Invalid date format for activity_closed_at"        |
      | 2025-01-14T10:00:00Z  | invalid_date    | "Invalid date format for cutoff_timestamp"          |

  Scenario: Deduplication logic for multiple records with same usr_id, usr_name, date, activity_bucket
    Given multiple records in AWS.entity_activity_log with same usr_id, usr_name, activity_closed_at date, and subject
    And matching records in AWS.priority_weight_settings for subject
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then only distinct combinations of usr_id, usr_name, date, activity_bucket should be present in activity_final_log

  Scenario: Table creation as managed table
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then the activity_final_log table should be created as a managed table in purgo_databricks.purgo_playground

  Scenario: No additional metadata columns are present
    When the Databricks SQL script is executed
    Then the activity_final_log table should only contain the specified columns:
      | usr_id          |
      | usr_name        |
      | date            |
      | activity_bucket |
      | new_act_flag    |
